=== 1. Bits per pixel — computed, not asserted ===
================================================================

    BPP = bitrate / (width x height x frames_per_second)

    It normalises bitrate against how much picture is being produced per
    second, which is the only way to compare a 1080p60 target with a 4K30 one.
    Two streams at the same Mbps can be wildly different in quality per pixel.

    Worked, for the required 1080p60 at 12 Mbps:

      12,000,000 / (1920 x 1080 x 60)
        = 12,000,000 / 124,416,000
        = 0.0965 bpp

    resolution   fps        bitrate        bpp        verdict
    ----------   ---        -------        ---        -------
    1920x1080    30         6 Mbps         0.0965     in range
    1920x1080    30         8 Mbps         0.1286     in range
    1920x1080    30         12 Mbps        0.1929     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    30         20 Mbps        0.3215     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    30         35 Mbps        0.5626     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    30         50 Mbps        0.8038     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    60         6 Mbps         0.0482     too low - visible artefacts
    1920x1080    60         8 Mbps         0.0643     in range
    1920x1080    60         12 Mbps        0.0965     in range   <== THE REQUIREMENT
    1920x1080    60         20 Mbps        0.1608     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    60         35 Mbps        0.2813     wasteful - diminishing returns
    1920x1080    60         50 Mbps        0.4019     wasteful - diminishing returns
    3840x2160    30         6 Mbps         0.0241     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    30         8 Mbps         0.0322     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    30         12 Mbps        0.0482     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    30         20 Mbps        0.0804     in range
    3840x2160    30         35 Mbps        0.1407     in range
    3840x2160    30         50 Mbps        0.2009     wasteful - diminishing returns
    3840x2160    60         6 Mbps         0.0121     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    60         8 Mbps         0.0161     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    60         12 Mbps        0.0241     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    60         20 Mbps        0.0402     too low - visible artefacts
    3840x2160    60         35 Mbps        0.0703     in range
    3840x2160    60         50 Mbps        0.1005     in range

    The sane band for live HEVC is roughly 0.05 - 0.15 bpp:

      below ~0.05   blocking and mosquito noise on motion; the encoder runs
                    out of bits before it runs out of detail
      0.05 - 0.15   the working range for contribution and high-quality
                    distribution
      above ~0.15   diminishing returns. HEVC's efficiency means the extra
                    bits buy progressively less visible improvement, and the
                    uplink cost is linear

    12 Mbps at 1080p60 = 0.0965 bpp sits almost exactly mid-band. It is a
    defensible number rather than a round one, and it satisfies the stated
    >= 12 Mbps requirement without overshooting into waste.

    Note the asymmetry the table exposes: 12 Mbps at 4K60 is 0.0241 bpp, a
    QUARTER of the 1080p60 value and far below anything usable. The same
    "12 Mbps" is generous at one resolution and unusable at another, which is
    exactly why the requirement needs a BPP justification attached.

